## Advent at spa pace

The great advantage of advent in a spa town is that the market is not the main programme — it is the bonus. Mornings belong to treatments, afternoons to a walk, early evenings to the colonnade and a cup of mulled wine. December is also one of the quieter months: shorter waits for treatments, calmer pools and wellness areas, and more of the staff's time for each guest. If you have ever considered a [winter cure](/en/magazine/winter-in-marienbad), advent is its most attractive version.

The springs flow all year, regardless of the weather. Their composition and temperature do not change in December, and a drinking cure in a steamy colonnade while snow falls outside has a particular charm. What matters is this: **the drinking cure is not self-service.** Which spring, how much and for how long is always decided by a spa physician on the basis of your diagnosis. Without that consultation, treat the cup at the spring as part of the atmosphere rather than as treatment.

Accommodation in advent ranges from three-star houses to the five-star Nové Lázně (New Baths), which contains the Roman Baths of 1896. Many spa houses have a spring piped into the building, so that in a frosty week both the drinking cure and the treatments stay under one roof. An overview of the whole festive season is gathered in the [Christmas and New Year](/en/christmas-and-new-year) section.

## What to do on weekdays

Between advent weekends the town does not close — it merely slows down. Good options include:

- **Walking the promenades and the forest.** The Slavkov Forest begins a few hundred metres behind the colonnade, and its winter paths are among the quietest places in Bohemia. Shoes with grip and a head torch are worth packing — it gets dark shortly after four.
- **Cafés and restaurants.** December is the month of the long afternoon in a coffee house; our selection is in the article on the [best restaurants and cafés](/en/magazine/best-restaurants-cafes-marienbad).
- **Concerts and culture.** Advent concerts, the West Bohemian Symphony Orchestra and church music fill the weekdays as well — the current overview is in the [Culture](/en/culture) section.
- **Day trips.** Cheb, Loket and Karlovy Vary all hold their own advent markets and are comfortable half-day outings; see the article on [day trips from Marienbad](/en/magazine/day-trips-from-marienbad).

One thing not to plan for: **the Singing Fountain is out of operation in winter.** Its season ends on 31 October and does not resume until late April. The colonnade is worth the walk without it — arguably more so under Christmas lights. There is more about the fountain in its [own article](/en/magazine/singing-fountain).

## An advent weekend, step by step

If you are coming for two days, there is nothing to invent — the town has its own timetable, and all you need to do is join it.

**Friday.** Arrive in the afternoon, check in, and take a first walk along the main street before the light goes. The market opens on Friday, but the Friday evening is the quietest of the weekend — ideal for a first mulled wine without a queue and for working out which stall is where.

**Saturday.** A treatment or the pool in the morning, a late breakfast around midday, and the main programme on the colonnade in the afternoon: Saturday has the strongest line-up and the largest crowd. Early evening belongs to the lit colonnade — between five and seven the light is at its best. If you are travelling with children, plan Saturday around the St Nicholas celebration.

**Sunday.** A longer walk into the park or out to the Forest Spring in the morning, while there is still frost on the paths and nobody about. One last stop at the market before you leave — Sunday afternoon is the emptiest, and the stallholders have time to talk.

If you have three days, add a weekday rather than a second weekend. The difference between the colonnade on a Saturday and the colonnade on a Tuesday is precisely what people come to a spa town for.

## Practical tips

**When to come.** The first and second advent weekends have the fullest programme and the most festive atmosphere. The fourth weekend (18–20 December) is quieter and suits anyone who would rather walk towards Christmas than be swept into it. For complete silence, come midweek.

**Shopping over the holidays.** Czech law requires shops larger than 200 m² to close on 25 and 26 December, and to stop selling at midday on 24 December. Smaller shops and hotel services are exempt, but a large shop is best done earlier.

**What to wear.** The town sits 630 metres above sea level and December is markedly colder than in Prague. Snow is not guaranteed, but it is more likely than in the lowlands. Layers, waterproof shoes and a hat will noticeably extend your time at the market.

**Getting there.** The train from Prague is direct and the station is a few minutes from the spa quarter by trolleybus — the details are in [Train to Marienbad](/en/magazine/train-to-marienbad). Driving in winter means winter tyres, which are compulsory in the Czech Republic in winter conditions, and an electronic vignette for the motorway; parking within the spa zone is limited, and the [practical information](/en/practical-info) section explains the options.

**Booking.** Advent weekends and the days between the holidays are in demand, while December weekdays are considerably quieter. If you have a particular house or a stay with a treatment programme in mind, it makes sense to arrange it early — not out of urgency, but because the spa houses plan their December schedules well in advance too.

## Who advent in Mariánské Lázně suits

Couples who would take two calm days over one frantic afternoon of shopping. Families for whom St Nicholas on the colonnade will mean more than a crowded shopping centre. And above all, anyone who wants festive atmosphere combined with something that outlasts the presents — rest, movement and looking after their health.

Advent here does not last an evening. It lasts four weeks, and between the weekends the town offers its guests exactly the calm it offers for the rest of the year. If you would like to stay until the turn of the year, the [New Year's Eve and New Year programme](/en/magazine/new-years-eve-in-marianske-lazne-quiet-and-festive-celebrations) picks up where advent leaves off — and in between lies the quietest week a spa town has to offer.
